// =============================================================================
// verum_registry.node.search — one question, three answers
// =============================================================================
// Spec: registry-federation-protocol §5 (discovery)
//
// The policy layer over `protocol/query`. The rules say what a query
// MEANS; this says how a node runs one against what it holds, and what
// it is allowed to say about the result.
//
// THE DEMONSTRATION, and it is the reason this module is worth reading:
// the three functions below share ONE definition of the filter chain.
// Each consumes it into a different accumulator — a list, a count, a
// boolean — and none of them restates the query. That is not a
// convention anyone has to keep; it is what `Transducer`'s rank-2
// `fn<R>(Reducer<B, R>) -> Reducer<A, R>` makes possible and a rank-1
// signature would forbid.
//
// The alternative is three filter chains that agree today. A count that
// disagrees with the list it counts is a bug nobody sees until it is
// someone else's incident, and the language can rule it out at the type
// level rather than in review.
//
// A LIMITATION THE LANGUAGE HAD, and the record of it is worth keeping
// because of how it was found. The project-wide `verum check` refused
// these three calls with
//
//     expected 'Item<ListIter<Verified>>', found 'Verified'
//
// while the same file checked clean on its own and the programme ran and
// printed three agreeing answers. `Item<ListIter<Verified>>` IS
// `Verified`, so the checker was refusing working code — in the mode
// that gates everything.
//
// The natural spelling stayed instead of being worked around, and this
// file became the reproduction: a plain `ListIter`, no adapter chain,
// three functions differing only in their accumulator, which is smaller
// than anything the projection-normalisation task had. The root turned
// out not to be a missing reduction at all — the resolver was called and
// answered correctly in the same run that reported the mismatch, and the
// comparison simply did not ask it. Fixed at the comparison; these three
// functions check clean now.
//
// The rule this leaves behind: when a programme refuses to work around
// the language, the workaround it did not write is the reproduction
// somebody else needed.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, deliberately: it never contacts an upstream.
// Search answers from what this node HOLDS, so its answer is a fact
// about this node and not a claim about the world. `cache.lookup` is
// where reachability enters, and it is a different question with a
// different failure mode.

@arch_module(
    foundation: Foundation.ZfcTwoInacc,
    stratum: MsfsStratum.LFnd,
    lifecycle: Lifecycle.Definition
)
module verum_registry.node.search;

mount verum_registry.protocol.evidence.{Verified};
mount verum_registry.protocol.query.{Clause, Query, selector};
mount verum_registry.node.cache.{Cache};

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What a node holds, as a sequence
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Everything this node holds, in one list.
///
/// The cache is indexed by NAME because a lookup by coordinate must be
/// one probe; a search is the other shape, and it walks. Both are right
/// for their question, and pretending one structure serves both is how
/// a registry ends up scanning for a lookup or indexing for a scan.
///
/// A second index — by authority, by role — belongs here when a query
/// shape earns it, and it earns it when a measurement says so rather
/// than when someone anticipates it.
public fn held(cache: &Cache) -> List<Verified> {
    let mut out: List<Verified> = List.new();
    for entry in cache.entries.values() {
        out.push(entry.verified.clone());
    }
    out
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One selector, three consumers
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Every coordinate the query admits.
///
/// What a resolver asks for.
public fn find(cache: &Cache, q: &Query) -> List<Verified> {
    let all = held(cache);
    all.iter().transduce(selector(q)).collect()
}

/// How many the query admits.
///
/// What a health check asks for, and it does not build the list. The
/// same selector, a counting accumulator — no second filter chain, so
/// this figure cannot drift from `find`'s length.
public fn count(cache: &Cache, q: &Query) -> Int{it >= 0} {
    let all = held(cache);
    all.iter().transduce(selector(q)).fold(0, |n, _v| n + 1)
}

/// Whether the query admits anything at all.
///
/// What an availability probe asks. Stops at the first match — the
/// accumulator is a boolean and the traversal has nothing left to learn
/// once it is true.
public fn any(cache: &Cache, q: &Query) -> Bool {
    let all = held(cache);
    all.iter().transduce(selector(q)).fold(false, |seen, _v| true)
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The obligation this module carries (spec §6)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The count agrees with the list it counts.
///
/// This is the property the shared selector exists to guarantee, so it
/// is worth stating as a claim rather than trusting to the fact that
/// both functions were written on the same afternoon. If someone later
/// "optimises" `count` into its own filter chain, this is what stops
/// compiling.
public fn count_agrees_with_find(cache: &Cache, q: &Query) -> Bool {
    count(cache, q) == find(cache, q).len()
}

/// And `any` agrees with both.
public fn any_agrees_with_count(cache: &Cache, q: &Query) -> Bool {
    any(cache, q) == (count(cache, q) > 0)
}
